    <item rdf:about="https://hybridsky.org/w/recipes/flan?rev=1262359485&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2010-01-01T10:24:45-0400</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>Anonymous (anonymous@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>recipes:flan</title>
        <link>https://hybridsky.org/w/recipes/flan?rev=1262359485&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Flan

Oven 350 degrees

	*  6 eggs
	*  2 cups milk
	*  1 teaspoon vanilla extract
	*  1.5 cups sugar

	*  Caramelize half cup sugar in nonstick skillet, stirring constantly. When melted, pour into the bottom of a Pyrex dish.
		*  Beat 6 eggs, or mix in blender. Add milk, vanilla and 1 cup sugar. Beat thoroughly; pour into dish on top of caramelized sugar.</description>
    </item>
    <item rdf:about="https://hybridsky.org/w/recipes/gado_gado?rev=1625791090&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2021-07-08T20:38:10-0400</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>Anonymous (anonymous@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>recipes:gado_gado</title>
        <link>https://hybridsky.org/w/recipes/gado_gado?rev=1625791090&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Gado-Gado

By Hetty McKinnon (in NYT) 7-8-21
YIELD6 to 8 servings
TIME45 minutes

Gado-gado is a beloved dish across Indonesia. Each region has a different spin: In Jakarta, it is a “double-carb” dish, featuring both potato and lontong (rice cakes). In West Java, it is known as lotek atah or karedok and served with raw vegetables. At the heart of any gado-gado is the spicy peanut sauce: Some versions call for tamarind, lime, terasi (shrimp paste) or coconut milk. Others use peanut butter instead…</description>
    </item>
